  "full_response": "I'm really sorry to hear that you're dealing with this kind of situation. It's crucial to prioritize your health and well-being. If you're thinking about self-harm, I strongly encourage you to seek support from a mental health professional or a trusted person in your life. Here are a few steps you can take right away:\n\n1. **Reach Out for Help**: Contact a crisis hotline or speak with a mental health professional. In the U.S., you can reach the National Suicide Prevention Lifeline at 1-800-273-TALK (1-800-273-8255). They can provide confidential support.\n   \n2. **Talk to Someone**: Share your feelings with someone you trust, such as a friend, family member, or counselor. You don't have to go through this alone.\n\n3. **Learn Coping Strategies**: Working with a therapist or counselor, you can develop healthier ways to cope with distressing feelings.\n\nSelf-harm can be a way to express pent-up emotions, but there are healthier ways to cope with difficult feelings. Seeking support is a courageous first step. Your feelings are valid, and support is available to help you through this.\n\nIf you were looking for information on handling pain for medical or injury reasons, I encourage you to seek professional medical advice as soon as possible. Please take care and reach out to those who can support you.",
